tics that we use to  treat  both localized and advanced cancers are  not exclusive  for damaging if you will the tumor cell  they also have effects on the  microenvironment  a number of studies have shown that  damage responses in the microenvironment  usually due to genotoxic stress dna  damaging agents like radiation platinum  chemotherapy and others  can induce a secretory program from that  micro environment  these include went signaling factors  much regulated by nf kappa b it also  influences the immune cell in the immune  environment which  in some cases could promote therapy  resistance and tumor growth  so these microenvironment features  are ideally suited to be assayed  by digital spatial profiling  can you compare and contrast the dsp  analysis of transcripts  versus protein  right so comparing assay  of transcripts and protein by dsp  one of the the major advantages of dsp  is  essentially within the same tissue  sample or the same  tumor sample you can acquire both  protein information  and transcript information so  the transcripts currently  are allowing for many more genes to be  acid  you can certainly multiplex proteins in  the data that i  showed we're looking at about 60  proteins  one of the challenges that each antibody  has to be  tagged specifically and worked up to  show that it's going to perform exactly  the same way  as a non-tagged antibody so some of the  preservatives need to be accounted for  in terms of the antibody  but clearly protein levels don't always  equate to transcript levels  and they're certainly things that we're  missing at the transcript level that may  be  altered at the protein level some say  the major difference currently is being  able to sample many many more genes  by the transcript based assays  potentially the entire transcriptome  whereas proteins at this point require a  good antibody  but using both assays simultaneously  certainly gives you  both higher confidence in any individual  measurement  and also provides some complementary  information where transcripts for  example may not equate  to expression of a particular protein  either in the tumor or the micro  environment  are there issues with particular samples  for example the age  or an ffpe tissue block  so there clearly are issues with  particular samples  we were fortunate to be able to use this  rapid autopsy program for our analysis  and we clearly excluded certain samples  that  for example had severe necrosis within  them  but we did show that dating back about  10 years  these samples that were formal and fixed  and archived  still had a very suitable nucleic acid  and protein for the dsp-based  analysis and that's something that we  wouldn't have been able to  evaluate at the like using a technology  such as rna-seq  we don't know if even older ffpe  tissue blocks would be suitable they  very well may be but  we don't know at this point in time but  at least in our case  dating back eight to ten years they were  perfectly  suitable for dsp bone is still a  challenge no doubt  um the samples that we looked at overall  had lower signal  uh for the transcript profiles likely  due  to the processing that needed to be done  for decalcification  but there are some methods where you  don't need to  use as harsh of conditions for decal  and so i would anticipate that those  samples would  be a better quality even than the ones  that we use so  i certainly wouldn't rule out bone as a  as a routine analyte  but they should be considered carefully  are there spatial aspects to immune  cells in prostate metastasis that may  influence tumor behaviors  or treatment responses  right so um we don't really know at this  point  whether there is a spatial influence  for immune cells in the metastases  that's a very important very interesting  question  i think that has been assessed in  other tumor types where you actually do  see  robust immune responses for example two  to immune checkpoint blockade for  example in melanoma bladder cancer and  some lung cancers  the problem is in prostate cancer we  have yet to see  consistent robust responses with the  exceptions of those  tumors that have high mutation burdens  due to mismatch repair deficiencies so  i think once we start to develop  cohorts of patients that we do see  responses to  with ideally uh a new  immune checkpoint uh blockade strategy  or  uh car t cell-based methods that we will  be able to  assess the role of or the response of  the spatial  contribution to treatments  so that's an active area of work and as  i mentioned  in my talk we haven't yet  done a rigorous analysis of the  peritumoral  composition which could be related to  prostate cancer phenotype or the  subtypes and potentially to treatment  responses so that's  ongoing work that we hope to complete  the next six to 12 months  so i appreciate your attention  what i tried to demonstrate here and  show  to the digital spatial profiling is that  advanced or metastatic prostate cancer  clearly has different phenotypes with  spatial  influences that may be important in  understanding  disease progression in response to  therapy  and the striking lack of immune cells  within these  metastases so this really opens up  the opportunity for future work in  enhancing immune responses  and also taking into account tumor  heterogeneity as we consider different  combinations of therapies for advanced  cancers  so i want to thank all of the my  colleagues  the people that actually did the work  related to these studies at fred hutch  the university of washington and nana  string  and again thanks for your attention
 

